This post has come the closest to convincing me that Nethack is truly
a "different" class of game.  However, what comes to mind, regarding
this argument of not being similar to a "graded or "advancing" manner
of gameplay, is this:  the key to the nature of Nethack is the RNG. In
essence, it's more like a game of poker than a game of chess, because
in chess the variables are always precisely the same, and you can then
improve your skills in manipulating those variables.  Whereas in NH,
even if you do develop skills in combat technique, inventory
management, scroll/spell usage, it still comes down to the cards you
are dealt, so to speak.  A skillful payer can play the odds for a
winning hand in poker, but not when they're dealt rubbish to start.
However, with bluffing and attentiveness, victory can still be had.

I don't know what this all has to do with the arument for/against
savescumming, except to say that in a game of poker you can't "do
over" a bad play!  But I still find odd, awkward, and just a little
disconcerting the obsession with a "one strike and your out" style of
play.  Except for someone like the world's best Pacman player, who
played the game to its logical and phyisical ending by necessarily
playing only his first Pacman, the paradigm for videogames has been
"lather, rinse, repeat" since Pong and Space Invaders.  I'm not trying
to convince anyone here, so you don't have to get upset with me, just
thinking out loud.  But I would be interested in knowing how other
players defend the "one strike and you're out" gameplay.  Perhaps it's
supposed to mirror the harsh reality of "real life", for all of us who
lack real risk in our lives ;^)?


